/// A Wi-Fi network in range, from the last scan; access
/// points of one network name are one entry (the strongest). Keyed by
/// `ssid`.
record AccessPoint key ssid {
  /// The network's name.
  ssid: text
  /// Signal strength, 0 to 1.
  strength: float
  /// It needs a password: a WPA, WPA2 or WPA3 personal or a WEP network,
  /// which `connect_with` joins (a network menu's password field). An
  /// open or Enhanced Open network takes none, and an enterprise
  /// (802.1X) one joins through its saved profile, with `connect()`.
  secure: bool
  /// It is the network joined (`network.ssid`); not while it is still
  /// being joined or being left (`network.connecting`,
  /// `network.disconnecting`).
  active: bool
  /// Joins it: with its saved connection if there is one, else a new one
  /// (for a `secure` network NetworkManager asks its secret agent for the
  /// password; iwd joins only a network it knows this way); an enterprise
  /// (802.1X) network with no saved profile is refused (it is set up in
  /// the system's network settings, or iwd's provisioning file). A failure,
  /// including one NetworkManager reports later (no password, a wrong
  /// one, a timeout), is `network.failed`.
  action connect()
  /// Joins a `secure` network with `password` (its WPA key or WEP key),
  /// saved in its connection: a network menu's password field. On any
  /// other network it fails. A failure is `network.failed`.
  action connect_with(password: text)
}

/// The network, followed live: from NetworkManager, or from iwd
/// (`net.connman.iwd`) where NetworkManager does not run. Either one
/// starting or stopping switches to the other. `network.disconnect()`
/// leaves the Wi-Fi network joined, and `network.scan()` scans for
/// networks now.
service network {
  /// A network connection is up. Over iwd, which knows only Wi-Fi: the
  /// Wi-Fi network is joined.
  connected: bool
  /// The Wi-Fi network joined, if any.
  ssid: text?
  /// The Wi-Fi signal strength, 0 to 1.
  strength: float
  /// The Wi-Fi radio is on. Writable.
  wifi: bool rw
  /// The Wi-Fi networks in range, keyed by `ssid`. Scanned only while a
  /// visible reader reads them (a network menu that is open).
  access_points: [AccessPoint]
  /// NetworkManager or iwd answers: false without either (or without a
  /// system bus), so a menu can hide what cannot work.
  available: bool
  /// The Wi-Fi network being joined, by name: from the moment
  /// `ap.connect()` or `ap.connect_with()` is asked, or the daemon starts
  /// joining one on its own, until it is joined or fails; null otherwise
  /// (`if network.connecting == ap.ssid { … }`).
  connecting: text?
  /// The Wi-Fi network being left (`network.disconnect()`, or the daemon
  /// leaving it), until it is left; null otherwise.
  disconnecting: text?
  /// The joined Wi-Fi network's frequency in MHz (2412, 5180, 5955): below
  /// 3000 is the 2.4 GHz band, 5150 to 5895 the 5 GHz band, from 5925 the
  /// 6 GHz band. Null while no Wi-Fi network is joined, or when iwd does
  /// not say.
  frequency: int?
  /// How the joined Wi-Fi network is secured; null while none is joined.
  security: WifiSecurity?
  /// The connection's IP address, as text without a prefix: its IPv4
  /// address, else its global IPv6 address. Null while not connected.
  /// Over NetworkManager this is the default-route connection's interface
  /// (wired too); over iwd it is the Wi-Fi interface. Read from the
  /// kernel, and followed only while a visible reader reads it.
  address: text?
  /// Bytes a second received through the machine's network hardware
  /// (every interface backed by a device: Ethernet, Wi-Fi, a modem; not
  /// loopback, bridges, VPN tunnels or containers' links), from the
  /// kernel's counters whichever daemon runs: `graph network.rx {
  /// range: auto }`. Sampled once a second while a visible reader reads
  /// it or `tx`; while none does but a `graph` keeps history of the
  /// service, at the coarser of that and a twentieth of the graph's
  /// `history` (at least 2 s); otherwise not at all. 0 until the first
  /// second has passed.
  rx: float
  /// Bytes a second sent, as `rx` counts them.
  tx: float
  /// A Wi-Fi scan runs: one `scan()` asked, or the scan a network menu
  /// asks when it opens; over iwd, iwd's own scans too.
  scanning: bool
  /// Leaves the Wi-Fi network joined, or stops the one being joined; the
  /// daemon then joins nothing on its own until a network is joined again.
  /// A join it stops is no failure; if the disconnect itself fails, that
  /// join goes on and is reported as any join is. A `disconnect()` while
  /// one is in flight (a double click) is dropped. Without a network joined
  /// or being joined it fails. A failure is `network.failed`.
  action disconnect()
  /// Scans for Wi-Fi networks now (a refresh button): `scanning` while it
  /// runs, `access_points` read again when it ends. A scan the daemon is
  /// already running, or has just run, is not asked again and is no
  /// failure. With the radio off, or a Wi-Fi device that cannot scan (its
  /// hardware switch off, not ready), it fails, as
  /// `network.failed("", error)`.
  action scan()
  /// Joining a network failed (no password and no secret agent to ask
  /// for one, a wrong one, out of range, timed out, or the service not
  /// running): `on network.failed(ssid, error) { … }`. `ssid` is the
  /// network the action was about, "" for one that names none (`scan()`,
  /// or a `disconnect()` the service could not take).
  event failed(ssid: text, error: text)
}
